WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.924 --> 00:00:04.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.970
ZAK STONE: Welcome to
"Cloud OnAir," live webinars

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.170
from Google Cloud.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.780
We're hosting webinars
like this every Tuesday.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.520
I'm Zak Stone and
I'm here to talk

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.010
to you about supercomputers
for machine learning

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.570
called Cloud TPUs.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.010
You can ask questions at
any time on the platform.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.680
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.060
Let's get started.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.420
Here's a sneak peek
at three generations

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.580
of our Tensor
Processing Unit systems,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.740
or TPUs, that we're going
to be talking about today.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.500
But before we get
into the details,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.570
I'd just like to set a
little bit of context

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.409
about the tremendous
amount of progress

00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:43.950
in machine learning there's been
over the past several years,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.500
to explain why these
TPUs are so useful.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.320
There's a website called Archive
where people are publishing

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.512
papers in machine
learning out in the open,

00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:55.970
and what you can see here is
a plot of the number of papers

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.590
published over time.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.010
The amazing thing is, this
rate is tracking faster

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.620
than Moore's law, and we're
now up to more than 50 machine

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.990
learning papers
published every day.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.834
There's a tremendous amount
of activity in the field.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.250
And among those
many papers, there

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.250
have been tremendous
improvements in accuracy

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.790
on standard benchmarks such
as the ImageNet recognition

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.020
competition.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.900
You can see here off to the
left, the AlexNet paper that

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.440
was the first one to apply
these deep convolutional neural

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.200
networks with GPUs to
the ImageNet challenge.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.860
And then since then, there's
been tremendous innovation,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.890
both in the network
architectures, the systems

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.330
to train these
models, all the way up

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.390
and to the right here in 2017.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:43.280
And we've even exceeded
these accuracies now.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.360
But this isn't just
about benchmarks

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.010
for scientific computing.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.730
These new deep learning
models have also

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.970
brought about breakthroughs
in real world applications,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.050
such as you can see here
in diabetic retinopathy.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.590
So researchers at the medical
brain team here at Google

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.830
have been trying to diagnose
a leading cause of blindness

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.223
from taking these images
of the back of the eye.

00:02:07.223 --> 00:02:08.639
And they discovered
in the process

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:10.259
of doing so that
you can also get

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.090
early indications
of heart disease

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.010
in this entirely passive way.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.600
This wasn't previously known.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.280
And this is just
one small indication

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.870
of how these machine
learning models

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.050
are opening up new applications
in medicine and elsewhere

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.130
that are really going to
improve people's lives.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:29.900
Similarly, here, folks have
been repurposing old cell phones

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.570
as listening devices
in the rain forest

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:37.990
to help catch signs of illegal
logging in the central Amazon.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:44.060
So these applications are just
a sample of many, many new ways

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.100
that machine learning
can be applied now

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.590
that we're reaching these
high level of accuracies

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.390
across many different
domains ranging

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.830
from image recognition
to speech recognition

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.920
to machine translation and more.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.740
And one thing that I
found especially exciting

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.920
is that the same kinds of
mathematical techniques

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.430
are now used to achieve
state of the art results

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.740
across these many
different disciplines.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.260
When I was in grad school
in computer vision,

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.540
the techniques that we
used in computer vision

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.400
didn't relate at all
to the techniques that

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.691
were being used for state of
the art speech recognition

00:03:15.691 --> 00:03:18.320
at the time, or state of
the art machine translation.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.710
Now, even though the models
aren't necessarily the same,

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.070
we're finding that they're built
of the same building blocks--

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.100
the dense linear algebra,
the matrix multiplies,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.870
and vector operations that have
been put together in this field

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.410
called deep learning.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.760
And so what that
means is you can

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.760
use some of the same tools and
the same hardware platforms

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.070
to solve many
different problems.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.230
However, there is a challenge.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.650
As you can see here, going back
to that case study of image

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.680
recognition accuracy
improving over time,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.830
as you're getting to higher
and higher levels of accuracy,

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:59.060
you're also requiring
more and more computation.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.620
In this case, on
the x-axis, you're

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.140
seeing the number of
multiply add operations,

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.660
which is sort of a rough
measure of complexity

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:08.330
of the amount of
compute required

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.637
to run one of these models.

00:04:09.637 --> 00:04:11.720
But that also corresponds
to the amount of compute

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.820
that you're required
to train the model.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.680
And so we're continuing to
move up and to the right,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.240
but we're requiring more
computation to do it,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.670
both to train the model
and then to run that model

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.320
and make it useful.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.340
So how do we address
this challenge?

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.720
Well, we believe that the
answer is specialized hardware

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.960
for machine learning, such as
these tensor processing units.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.962
Here's a brief tour of the
history of TPUs at Google.

00:04:36.962 --> 00:04:38.420
This is the first
one that we built

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.180
that's been deployed in
data center since 2015

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.010
this one is focused
on inference,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.600
and you interacted
with one of these

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.460
whenever you ran a search.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.370
It would be used to re-rank the
last several hundred results

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.220
to choose those 10 blue links
that you get every time you

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.570
search on Google.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.240
It was used in Street
View, in Google Photos,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.730
in speech recognition,
and across a wide variety

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:03.050
of applications that you
interact with every day.

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:04.320
But that wasn't enough.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.130
We moved on after that to
the second generation tensor

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.790
processing unit, which was
built into these whole systems.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.940
Here, you can see a device that
we call the Cloud TPU that you

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.950
can rent today on Google Cloud.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.470
It's in beta, there's
no waiting, no sign up.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.310
You just follow the link
g.co/cloudtpu and fire up

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:25.130
a Cloud TPU, following
our quickstart.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:28.430
But these devices aren't
just standalone the way

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.510
the first TPU was.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.600
They're actually designed
to be connected together

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.200
into these giant
supercomputers called TPU pods.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.640
And these will be coming
to Cloud later this year,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.820
both in the larger
configurations

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:45.240
and in smaller slices that
you can use on demand.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.090
And the nice thing
about the TPU pod

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.720
is you can apply even more
computation to a single machine

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.720
learning problem to train
a model to the accuracy

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.687
that you desire faster.

00:05:55.687 --> 00:05:57.770
That helps you really
improve your iteration time.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.565
Because training a model is
sort of like compiling code

00:06:00.565 --> 00:06:01.940
in the world that
you're used to,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.800
of programming line by line.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.660
You have to do it
over and over again

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.820
to develop a good application.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.610
And at Google I/O, we
revealed that we're not just

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.450
stopping at TPUv2.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.620
We have a TPUv3, which
is a whole new chip built

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.420
into these whole
new systems that

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.430
are even larger
and more powerful

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:24.920
than the TPUv2 device and pod.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:27.200
So just to give you a
quantitative sense of how

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.440
fast this field is
moving and the lengths

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.751
we're going to to achieve
even greater amounts

00:06:32.751 --> 00:06:34.250
of machine learning
performance, you

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:39.290
can see that TPUv1 was
able to do 92 teraops,

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:43.700
whereas cloud TPU took
that to 180 teraflops

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.700
with 64 gigabytes of high
bandwidth memory per board,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.950
going from inference only
in the first generation

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:50.800
to supporting
training and inference

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.209
in the second generation.

00:06:52.209 --> 00:06:53.750
You stitch all those
together and you

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.180
can do even more than
11 and 1/2 petaflops

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.730
with 4 terabytes of HBM.

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:01.430
And now the third
generation pod breaks

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.590
the barrier of 100 petaflops.

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.140
More than 8x the
performance of a TPUv2

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:10.910
pod through both a new chip
and this larger scale system.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:14.000
So we're really committed
to expanding the AI frontier

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.670
and to sharing our
hardware platforms

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.460
and the software we run on
top of them, like TensorFlow,

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.310
with all of you
through Google Cloud.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.890
So I'd like to
focus on real world

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:30.300
performance with the Cloud
TPUs that you can rent today.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.830
Now, I know that one thing,
if you're training machine

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.170
learning models or running
machine learning models,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.142
performance is one of the most
important features for you.

00:07:39.142 --> 00:07:41.600
And I just want to emphasize
how important it is to measure

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.310
performance carefully.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.390
We try to emphasize
performance measurements that

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.400
are involving real
data, where you

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.020
have a real model
that's training

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.970
to a specific accuracy.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:57.700
And then you're measuring the
complete time to accuracy,

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.347
or cost to accuracy.

00:07:59.347 --> 00:08:01.430
If you read about performance
out on the internet,

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:04.000
you might see people talking
about images per second

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.260
or steps per second.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.840
And that's important,
but it's not useful

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.070
if you're just measuring
that in isolation,

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.340
because it's really
important to make sure

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:13.840
that the model that
you're measuring

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.800
that's processing, let's say,
lots of images per second,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.760
will actually get to the
destination you want to go,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.710
which is a specific accuracy on
a specific benchmark data set.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:25.960
So whenever you're
looking at benchmarks,

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.460
we really encourage you to look
for real world data, not just

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:33.130
synthetic data, for
time to accuracy,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.512
and also look for
total cost, especially

00:08:35.512 --> 00:08:37.720
via public clouds, where
it's easy to do these apples

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.475
to apples comparisons.

00:08:39.475 --> 00:08:42.100
We've also put a lot of emphasis
on making our machine learning

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.786
benchmarks reproducible
via open source,

00:08:44.786 --> 00:08:46.660
so you don't just have
to believe the numbers

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:48.190
that I'm sharing today.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:52.330
You can run our open source
code on Cloud TPUs and measure

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.640
results yourself, either
on standard benchmarks

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.130
or then on your own data.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:08:59.630
One benchmark
competition that we

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.210
think was especially well
designed was from Stanford.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:04.550
It's called DAWNBench,
and there's a URL here.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.640
This competition
recently concluded.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.390
And they looked at a
variety of data sets,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:11.450
including the standard
ImageNet benchmark

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.040
for image recognition.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.570
And they measured both
training time and total cost.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.540
And they also had other
competitions for inference.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.000
And we think that these
metrics were really

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.760
getting at the kinds of things
that you, as practitioners,

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.160
care about in your
day to day work.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.200
I'm happy to announce that we
had the number one DAWNBench

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.240
entry for ImageNet
training cost.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.339
And we achieved that with
this AmoebaNet-D model.

00:09:38.339 --> 00:09:40.130
And the interesting
thing about AmoebaNet-D

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:44.180
is the architecture was
actually built on a TPU

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.120
through an evolutionary
process automatically.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.590
So instead of
designing this model

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.951
by hand with countless
grad student hours,

00:09:51.951 --> 00:09:54.200
the way most of those models
that I referenced earlier

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.230
have been designed, we
ran a search process,

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.660
measuring a population
of models and choosing

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:02.480
the ones that performed best
on similar image recognition

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:03.650
tasks.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:06.950
Then we've open sourced
that final winning model,

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:10.070
AmoebaNet-D and you can run
it yourself on the Cloud TPU

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.240
platform today.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:14.960
Turns out, it trains to the
target accuracy of 93% top five

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.360
on ImageNet in just
7 and 1/2 hours,

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:19.919
for under $50,
which we think makes

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:22.460
these kinds of state of the art
machine learning results much

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:26.240
more affordable for everybody.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.030
There's also a model
called ResNet-50

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:31.240
that's often used as a benchmark
to compare different systems,

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.260
one to the other.

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:34.000
And so I should
just mention here

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.010
that our ResNet-50
implementation, which is also

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:40.540
open source, is able to
deliver 3,250 images a second

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.210
with real data, which
is really exciting,

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.090
and train to the similar
final accuracy of 93%,

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.740
top five, which is not easy to
achieve with a ResNet-50 model.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.430
That happens in
8.9 hours for $60.

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:57.710
And if you want
to go even faster,

00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.770
this is the advantage of
being able to scale up

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:01.780
into these pods.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.580
Here, you can see our
number one DAWNBench

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:07.750
entry with half of
a TPU pod, where

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:10.360
we were able to train
in just 30 minutes.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.480
And actually, if you eliminate
the overhead of checkpointing

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.330
to measure your
accuracy at every step,

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:21.910
the minimum time is just 23.9
minutes to get to that same 93%

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.540
accuracy target.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.810
So I'm really excited
about the fact

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.330
that you can develop on
one of these Cloud TPUs

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:31.570
and then soon, you'll be able to
scale up with no code changes,

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:34.780
just changing your batch size,
onto larger and larger slices

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:37.370
of these TPU pods.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:41.340
Now, I mentioned earlier
the number of archive papers

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.030
that are getting
published every day,

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:45.240
and there is real
progress happening there,

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:51.210
including new tricks that
sometimes change the algorithms

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:54.360
and make it possible to
achieve your goals even faster.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.610
One set of those tricks
came from an organization

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:00.330
called fast.ai, which published
a very strong third place entry

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.190
in the DAWNBench competition.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:05.580
They showed that if you
progressively scale up

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.100
the size of your training
images while you're training,

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:11.460
that can let you go even
faster in the early stages

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:12.769
of your training process.

00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:15.060
And they also came up with
a different, more aggressive

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.520
learning rate schedule that
wasn't widely known that

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.890
had been published previously.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:21.480
And they put these
together, and they

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.640
were able to train
the ResNet-50 model,

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.470
with this modified
training protocol,

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.500
to 93% accuracy much faster.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.030
Fortunately, because all this
is happening out in the open,

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:33.870
we were able to reproduce these
algorithmic changes quickly

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.320
on Cloud TPU after the DAWNBench
competition had closed,

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.740
and that takes our training
costs down from $59

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:43.210
to just $25, even at
on-demand pricing.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.510
So we think it's
really fantastic

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:47.640
that so many people are
out there experimenting

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:49.890
with different things
and that they're

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:51.600
sharing their
results in the open

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.080
so that you can benefit from
them immediately on Cloud

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.672
TPUs or other platforms.

00:12:57.672 --> 00:12:59.130
Now, you might
wonder, well, what's

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:03.370
the next benchmark challenge,
now that DAWNBench is over?

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:07.366
We recommend MLPerf, which
you can find at MLPerf.org.

00:13:07.366 --> 00:13:08.740
There's a number
of organizations

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.570
that are coming
together to define

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:13.270
an even newer and more
challenging and diverse

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.650
set of benchmarks.

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:16.327
And so this has been
announced recently,

00:13:16.327 --> 00:13:18.160
and there will be
deadlines later this year.

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.890
If you want to submit
your own entries,

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:24.390
I'd highly encourage
you to do so.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:28.130
So as I mentioned before, if
you want to experience the kind

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:31.460
of performance I'm talking
about today on these Cloud TPU

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:36.080
devices, 180 teraflops,
you can do so by visiting--

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:39.050
the simplest link
is g.co/cloudtpu,

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:41.570
but you can also follow
these other two links to see

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:43.850
our documentation, where
we have tutorials for many

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:46.760
of the models that I'll mention,
or you can just go straight

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.880
to GitHub and read the code
yourself for some of the state

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:53.810
of the art models that I
mentioned earlier today.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:56.760
Now, what's it like to
actually use these Cloud TPUs?

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.620
I want to give you more of a
sense of how these things work

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.190
and what it's like
to work with them.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.010
So one important point
is that Cloud TPUs

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.750
are network-attached.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.000
So this is different
from other accelerators.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.490
You can choose a VM of whatever
size or shape that you want,

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:14.580
which I'm showing here
in the middle here.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:18.270
The customer off to the
left is SSHed into that VM,

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.100
you can install whatever
software you want,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.590
you can attach other
accelerators if you want to,

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.840
you can choose a
custom size and shape.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:27.810
And then you install our
machine image that we provide,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:31.830
which has open source TensorFlow
on it, with TensorFlow 1.8,

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:35.280
and then this VM communicates
over the network with a Cloud

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.480
TPU.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.480
Now, this does add one
layer of interaction,

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.690
but we still try to provide
maximum flexibility and control

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.550
with this Cloud
TPU infrastructure.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.070
And the nice thing about
this network separation

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:50.460
is that Cloud TPU can either
be a single device, like I

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:51.960
was showing you
earlier, or it could

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:53.640
be a whole slice of a pod.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.090
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:56.880
There are no drivers to install.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.150
And you can use your
VM, which can be small,

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.490
to drive this
enormous supercomputer

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.970
to get your training jobs
done quickly and efficiently.

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:08.520
Now, as I've
mentioned throughout,

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:11.190
these Cloud TPUs today are
programmed with TensorFlow.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:13.140
And I'd recommend
using TensorFlow 1.8

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:16.597
to get our greatest
optimizations

00:15:16.597 --> 00:15:18.180
and the maximum
performance that we're

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.740
able to deliver on Cloud TPUs.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.490
Now, TensorFlow is a really
practical and popular tool kit.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.700
Ever since being open
sourced in November, 2015,

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:29.350
it's just really taken off.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.076
If you look at
these GitHub stars,

00:15:31.076 --> 00:15:32.450
they're just up
and to the right.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:36.160
I think this is now within the
top five most popular projects

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.300
on GitHub overall.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:42.310
And we've seen more than 13
million downloads of TensorFlow

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:43.580
all around the world.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:46.180
So this is really a
production ready framework

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.820
that's used in everything
from research labs

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:50.740
to applications that
you interact with that

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.280
have billions of users.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:55.980
Now, TensorFlow has
a whole ecosystem,

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.000
and I'm going to focus
today on the TF data API,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.060
on layers, estimators,
and XLA, because those

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:08.050
are the components that you use
to program these Cloud TPUs.

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:10.300
So estimators and layers
are these high level,

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:13.210
deep learning abstractions for
TensorFlow that you don't just

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:14.170
have to use on TPUs.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:16.210
You can use them on
other platforms as well.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:18.460
And we're trying to make it
easy for you to shift code

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:21.160
back and forth from one
platform to another,

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:23.720
so you're not locked in.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.590
Now, here's an example
of the kind of model

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:28.960
you might write with
layers and estimators.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:30.820
This is a convolutional model.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:34.920
And so you can run this
on your local computer.

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:38.680
And how do you change it to
make it compatible with a TPU?

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:41.140
Well, in this case, you
just have some small changes

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:41.950
to make.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.870
You wrap the optimizer
here, as you can see,

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.690
and then you're using
a TPU estimator.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:50.540
And we're working to minimize
these changes over time.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:52.600
Now, one thing I
want to say, though,

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.550
is that high
performance computing

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:57.010
is challenging on any platform.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:59.740
And so we've put a lot
of effort into building

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.560
a set of reference models
that are open source

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.680
that you can just use right away
as starting points so you don't

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:08.230
have to do this performance
optimization from scratch

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.619
yourself in these many
different application domains.

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:15.280
So to give you a sense of the
reference models, a snapshot

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:18.369
that we have today, we've got
the kinds of image recognition

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:21.800
models that I've already
discussed off to the left,

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.770
including the state of
the art AmoebaNet model.

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:28.569
We've also got various sides of
ResNets and inception models,

00:17:28.569 --> 00:17:30.670
of V2, V3, and V4.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:32.542
But we've also got
object detection

00:17:32.542 --> 00:17:34.000
with RetinaNet,
that helps you find

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.800
multiple objects in an image.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.990
We've got low resource
models like MobileNet,

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:41.020
if you want to then deploy
your model out at the edge,

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:44.230
on a phone or a vehicle
or some other device.

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.140
But these TPUs aren't just
limited to image recognition

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.490
and object detection.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:51.370
They're also useful for
machine translation or language

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:54.220
modeling, or even sentiment
analysis or question answering.

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.210
I'll say a little bit more
about that in a moment.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.690
And beyond language
and text, we're

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:01.910
seeing emerging
applications on these TPUs

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:03.650
in speech recognition,
which you can find

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:05.630
in the Tensor2Tensor toolkit.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.620
I'll say a bit more about that.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:09.260
And if you're more
of a researcher,

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:11.630
you're interested in
generating images from scratch,

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.580
and some of these
far out applications

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:15.830
where these machine
learning models

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:19.310
are synthesizing new types
of imagery and sound,

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:20.730
you can do those on TPU too.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:24.780
And we've got a great example
of an image transformer model.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:28.560
So let me go through some
of these applications

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:30.680
and this variety of
applications in turn.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.700
Image classification
typically are

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.440
focusing on small
crops of images

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.930
like you're seeing here, and
then applying a category.

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:38.820
This is useful for
everything from photo

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:41.854
sharing to medical imaging.

00:18:41.854 --> 00:18:43.270
Object detection,
like I mentioned

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:44.853
before, you might
have a larger image,

00:18:44.853 --> 00:18:46.660
like I'm showing here
off to the right.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:49.760
And you want to detect
a bunch of items in it

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:51.430
and classify them
and apply labels

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.050
with different confidences.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:54.820
In this case,
RetinaNet can operate

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:58.600
on surprisingly large
images, 896 by 896,

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:01.590
achieve a near state of
the art result pm COCO,

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.420
a standard object
recognition data set,

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:07.060
and you can get to
that level of accuracy

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:10.230
in about six hours for just
$40 on one of the Cloud TPUs

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.717
that you can rent today.

00:19:11.717 --> 00:19:14.050
I think that's pretty exciting
for democratizing machine

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:16.930
learning and providing
a building block for you

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:18.400
to build your own
object detection

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.230
applications on top of.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:24.190
In machine translation,
on the WMT data set

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.170
that translates
English to German,

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:29.980
we get to a blue score of 28.6
in just over six hours, again,

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:32.400
for around $40.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.050
For language
modeling, as you might

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:36.540
use for an application
like Smart Reply,

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.210
where you're trying to predict
what words or phrases happen

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:41.460
next, it costs a little
bit more because you

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.770
have to train longer to get
to the state of the art,

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:46.890
but you reach the state of
the art in just 74 hours.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:49.170
again, for under $500.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:51.000
And this, for the
latest hyper-parameters,

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.550
I'd recommend following the
short link on screen now.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.130
That takes you to the
Tensor2Tensor toolkit, which

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:58.110
is built on top of
TensorFlow, and is

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:01.860
moving even faster and really
keeping up with, or extending,

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.750
the state of the
art in research.

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:06.580
For speech recognition,
which you can also

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:11.080
find in Tensor2Tensor, you can
get to a word error rate of 7.9

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:13.390
in just 13 hours for $86.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:14.980
Or you can train
longer to get even

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:19.002
closer to the state of the art
with a 7.3% word error rate.

00:20:19.002 --> 00:20:20.710
And this is a really
great starting point

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:23.170
for processing large
volumes of audio

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:25.840
or training your robot to
listen to your voice commands

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:29.180
or anything else
you'd like to do.

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:31.160
I'd also like to
emphasize that even

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:33.530
techniques that you
wouldn't necessarily think

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.140
are a good fit for accelerators,
like question answering,

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:37.070
can be accelerated.

00:20:37.070 --> 00:20:41.090
Here's a QANet where you
take in a paragraph of text,

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:43.130
some region of
unstructured text,

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.700
and you're asked a question
that has an answer in that text.

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:49.700
And this QANet will
highlight the fact

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:51.560
in this unstructured
blob of text

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.550
that corresponds to the answer.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:57.230
This is a fast implementation
that won the DAWNBench SQuAD

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:58.287
challenge.

00:20:58.287 --> 00:21:00.620
And so there's going to be
an open source implementation

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:02.600
of this coming soon.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.790
Now, I mentioned image
generation earlier.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:06.800
You can see off to the
right in these rows

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:08.780
of different
categories, these images

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:11.990
have actually been synthesized
from scratch using the image

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:13.640
transformer model.

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:18.920
And using a Cloud TPU, you can
get to a score of 2.98 bits per

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:22.040
dimension-- lower is
better in this case--

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:24.447
in 30 hours for under $200.

00:21:24.447 --> 00:21:26.030
I've also provided
a link to the paper

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.730
here if you're interested
in learning more.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:31.400
So I've mentioned this
variety of reference models,

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.500
and there are more
coming all the time.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.990
I'd really encourage you--
even though it's tempting,

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:38.895
to take some older code and
try and port it to the TPU--

00:21:38.895 --> 00:21:41.270
I'd really recommend starting
with one of these reference

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:45.440
models to experience the full
performance of the platform

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:46.370
right away.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:47.960
And maybe even reach
higher accuracy,

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:51.094
since these models are so
close to the state of the art.

00:21:51.094 --> 00:21:53.010
They're high performance,
they're open source,

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:54.180
they're cutting edge.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:56.960
We continuously test them
for performance and accuracy,

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.630
even as all the versions
of underlying software

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:01.640
are evolving rapidly.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:03.860
And they really let you
get up and running quickly.

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:04.980
And then, it's your code.

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:05.730
It's open source.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.430
You can modify it as
you need and train

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:10.050
and run on your own data.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:13.100
So I want to emphasize, though,
that you don't just have

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:15.074
to use these reference models.

00:22:15.074 --> 00:22:16.490
You can build
models from scratch,

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:19.760
using layers and estimators,
and the TF data API.

00:22:19.760 --> 00:22:21.897
And there's lots of
detailed technical advice

00:22:21.897 --> 00:22:23.480
in this troubleshooting
guide and also

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:26.931
in our performance guide and
our documentation of help tools.

00:22:26.931 --> 00:22:29.180
But we're really trying to
do the hardest work for you

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.710
via our reference models.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:33.560
So definitely,
start there today.

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:36.200
Just to give you one quick
sense of why high performance

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:38.210
computing can be so
hard, when you're

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:42.050
using previous accelerators
that may not have been as fast,

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:45.902
it's easy to overlap your input
processing with your training.

00:22:45.902 --> 00:22:48.110
And so then you're fully
utilizing your accelerators,

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:50.700
which are often the most
expensive part of the system.

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.060
But now, when the
accelerators get faster,

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.460
suddenly you're bottlenecked
on the input processing

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:57.920
and your accelerators are
idle, waiting for data.

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:00.770
It's quite challenging to build
these balanced systems that

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:03.170
can keep the fastest
accelerators supplied

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:04.600
with data all the time.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:06.183
And so that's really
what we're trying

00:23:06.183 --> 00:23:08.300
to do with our
TPU supercomputers

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:12.880
and also with all these software
layers that we build on top.

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:15.100
Fortunately, if you are
performance tuning one

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:17.050
of these models, we've
got these great tools

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:18.670
that are integrated
with TensorBoard

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:21.580
and available for Cloud TPUs.

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.310
The Cloud TPU profiler
lets you actually

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:27.804
get detailed op by op
statistics about how

00:23:27.804 --> 00:23:30.220
your models are performing and
where they're bottlenecked.

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:31.900
And this is a
really amazing tool

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.880
to let you optimize
your own models

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:40.610
and get them running,
training at full potential.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.540
We even try to give
you high level advice.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:44.160
I don't know if you can
read it here in red.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.160
But this is telling you
that your program is not

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:46.880
input bounded.

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:48.680
So keep working
on your step time.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.930
We're really trying to provide
higher and higher level

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:55.450
guidance as you're optimizing
these models on the TPU.

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:58.360
So let me just say
for a moment why

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:01.520
we recommend doing machine
learning in the cloud--

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:04.004
how that's providing
advantages to you,

00:24:04.004 --> 00:24:05.920
even if you're used to
working under your desk

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:08.550
or in an on premise data center.

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:11.520
Google Cloud provides
top notch infrastructure,

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.650
and you can mix and
match whatever components

00:24:13.650 --> 00:24:14.940
that you need.

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.022
You can provision
quickly, you can scale up,

00:24:17.022 --> 00:24:19.230
and then you can scale back
down as your needs change

00:24:19.230 --> 00:24:22.140
and really burst to take
advantage of teraflops

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.566
or even petaflops of
machine learning compute.

00:24:25.566 --> 00:24:27.690
We've also got a variety
of different accelerators.

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:31.080
We've got the best
of CPUs, GPUs, TPUs.

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:33.240
You can mix and
match those together

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.370
to get the right mix
for each workload

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:37.120
that's interesting to you.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.720
We've got fantastic security,
world class security,

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:42.510
and powerful tools for
data access control

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:45.090
and controlling the way
that it's being used.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:48.810
And most importantly, we
let you reduce your capital

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:49.680
expenditure.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.020
Instead of having to
place orders for hardware,

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:55.232
wait for months, deploy
them, change the cooling

00:24:55.232 --> 00:24:56.940
and power in your data
centers to support

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:59.850
these powerful
accelerators, you can just

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:01.920
use our on demand
pricing to pay only

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:04.740
for what you need, which we
find to be much more economical,

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:08.100
especially when your
workloads are unpredictable.

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:10.110
Just to give you two
examples of customers

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:13.650
who are using Cloud TPUs
today, both Two Sigma and Lyft

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:15.450
have found it really
convenient to be

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:18.410
able to move to the cloud, take
models that they already had,

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:20.940
in some cases, and accelerate
them, or take our reference

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.640
models and use them
to achieve new results

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:27.760
on their own internal data sets.

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:31.259
Now, I'll give you just a
brief glimpse under the hood

00:25:31.259 --> 00:25:33.550
to give you a little bit more
detail on how these Cloud

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:37.060
TPUs are able to achieve
such amazing performance.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:38.920
Now, I mentioned before
that each Cloud TPU

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:41.890
delivers 180 teraflops
of computation

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:43.810
with 64 gigabytes of memory.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:45.670
And it's actually
got four chips, one

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:47.380
under each of those heat sinks.

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:49.720
And so here, in
just a minute, I'm

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:51.790
going to focus on
one of those chips.

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.025
But first, let me
explain that the Cloud

00:25:54.025 --> 00:25:59.200
TPU is connected to a host,
behind the scenes, via PCI.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:02.620
So you get the benefit
of both this host, where

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:04.900
some operations can
be placed, and then,

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:07.180
ideally, as much of the
computation as possible

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:09.200
is placed on the accelerator.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:13.280
So let's zoom in on
one of these TPUs.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:16.090
So the Cloud TPU chip is
divided into two cores,

00:26:16.090 --> 00:26:17.470
as you can see here.

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.240
The chip as a whole
has 16 gigabytes

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:22.120
of HBM split between the cores.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:27.070
And each core delivers
22 and 1/2 teraflops

00:26:27.070 --> 00:26:30.400
and consists of a scalar unit,
a vector unit, and especially

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:34.210
this matrix unit, which is
really special and uniquely

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:37.990
suited for the demands of high
performance machine learning.

00:26:37.990 --> 00:26:40.420
Now, focusing on
this matrix unit,

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:44.120
it's actually 128 by
128 systolic array.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.870
Now, systolic arrays are this
old idea in chip architecture,

00:26:46.870 --> 00:26:48.610
but they're newly
relevant now that we

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:52.540
have these deep learning models
that have many, many layers

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:55.030
and consist of many
matrix operations,

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.580
often on these
gigantic matrices.

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:00.040
One important fact
about our matrix unit

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.860
is that we're actually using
a different floating point

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:05.950
format, bfloat16
in the multiplies,

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:08.222
even though we're doing
float32 accumulate.

00:27:08.222 --> 00:27:10.180
And that turns out to be
a great floating point

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:11.580
format for machine learning.

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:14.530
It's almost a drop in
replacement for Float32.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:16.090
And yet it lets
us pack even more

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.820
compute into the same
amount of silicon area.

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:22.450
So let me tell you just a
little bit more about bfloat16.

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:25.300
So you may be used to
programming in float32.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:26.980
That's what most
people use today,

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:28.330
for machine learning models.

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:31.450
And here's how those
32 bits are distributed

00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:34.450
between the exponent
and the mantissa.

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:38.380
Now, IEEE float16 chops
both of those down.

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:41.380
So you have a much reduced
exponent and a much reduced

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:42.880
mantissa.

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:47.440
Bfloat16, by contrast,
actually uses a lot more bits

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.200
for the exponent, which lets
us represent numbers that still

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:54.010
have this full range, but you
lose a little bit of detail

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:56.410
in fine distinctions
between numbers.

00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:58.300
Turns out, for all of
these state of the art

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:01.310
deep learning models, those
fine distinctions don't matter.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:02.920
But the range matters a lot.

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:06.250
And so you get a much
simpler user experience

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:09.622
working with bfloat16 than you
do having to worry about fp16,

00:28:09.622 --> 00:28:11.080
where you've got
to think carefully

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:14.050
about whether the numbers are
in the right ranges, everywhere

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:15.010
throughout your model.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:16.510
And there are
complicated techniques

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:19.450
that you have to apply
on a model by model basis

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:23.170
to avoid your numbers exploding
or under flowing or all sorts

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:24.730
of numerical problems.

00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:28.120
We reduce those problems a
lot by working with bfloat16

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:30.590
on the Cloud TPU.

00:28:30.590 --> 00:28:32.900
So now here, I just
want to show you a three

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:36.560
by three toy example to
represent this 128 by 128

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:40.250
systolic array, to give you a
sense of how, as input data is

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:43.550
streaming through, there
can be a lot of reuse

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:47.540
of intermediate computations
inside the systolic array,

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:49.730
which gives this
fantastic throughput

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:53.960
for these large
matrix operations.

00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:56.270
Now, I just want to
look ahead briefly

00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:59.300
to give you a sense of where
we think the field is going,

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:03.290
and why these Cloud TPUs and TPU
pods are so well-suited to help

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:08.210
support the future of machine
learning products and research.

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:10.790
Now, these Cloud
TPU pods enable you

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:15.410
to train with very large batch
sizes, which you might wonder,

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:20.720
well, how does the scaling go as
you go beyond a single device?

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:23.750
Fortunately, we've got this
proprietary network that

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:26.450
connects all these devices
together in this mesh,

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:30.530
and we've been able to show near
perfect linear scaling out all

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:32.930
the way to the full scale
pod, as you can see here,

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:35.840
as the number of devices
increases to 64, which is

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:41.190
the full set in the TPUv2 pod.

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:42.984
Now, I mentioned large batches.

00:29:42.984 --> 00:29:45.150
One other issue you have
to wonder about when you're

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.010
dealing with large
batch training

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:49.590
is whether you can still
make efficient progress

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.420
towards the ultimate goal.

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.176
Previously, as people increased
the batch size, at some point,

00:29:54.176 --> 00:29:56.550
you would find that you wouldn't
reach the final accuracy

00:29:56.550 --> 00:29:57.430
that you were expecting.

00:29:57.430 --> 00:29:59.940
And so there was sort of a
mathematical limit in how large

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:02.370
you could make the batch
size before it was no longer

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:04.464
efficient or useful to do so.

00:30:04.464 --> 00:30:06.630
But there's been a lot of
progress on this recently,

00:30:06.630 --> 00:30:08.670
and there's still
very active research.

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:11.880
And what I'm showing here is
that a model that might take,

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:17.910
in that sort of golden curve,
many hours on a single board

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:22.950
to get to the 76% accuracy, as
you scale up within the pod,

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:25.590
just increasing the batch
size, not changing the code,

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:27.690
you can achieve
that same accuracy

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.090
in shorter and shorter
wall clock times, which

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:31.470
really lets you iterate faster.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.150
Because remember, this is
like compiling your code.

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:34.650
Right?

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:36.780
You need to compile
your code many times

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:37.874
to iterate and explore.

00:30:37.874 --> 00:30:39.540
And sometimes, if
you're doing something

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.240
like hyper-parameter tuning or
neural architectures search,

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:44.130
you want to train
hundreds or even

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:45.870
thousands of different models.

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:49.560
And on these pods, you can
really pull that time in

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:52.560
to get to the same accuracy,
and iterate much more quickly

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.890
and make faster progress.

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:57.800
Now, here's just a glimpse
of our future roadmap.

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:03.590
I mentioned the TPUv3 pod that
we announced at Google I/O.

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:06.410
And here's the full system
with these eight racks.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:08.090
And the whole pod,
as I mentioned,

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:11.030
is more than eight times more
powerful than the TPU pod.

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:14.420
And actually, to get to
this level performance,

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:18.226
we brought liquid cooling
into our data centers

00:31:18.226 --> 00:31:19.850
for the first time,
as you can see here

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:23.960
on one of the boards that makes
up that gigantic TPUv3 pod.

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:27.680
So we're really excited
about continuing

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:30.210
to expand the frontier
of machine learning.

00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:32.210
You can get started
with Cloud TPUs today.

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.530
There's no waiting,
there's no sign up,

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:36.950
there's no need to
make a quota request.

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:41.270
Every GCP project can
start a Cloud TPU today.

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:43.880
And you can follow the
link at the bottom,

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:47.480
the g.co/cloudtpu
link to get started,

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:50.520
or one of these other links
to peruse the code of our open

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:55.370
source reference models, or jump
straight to our documentation.

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:56.750
With that, I'd
like to invite you

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:59.711
to stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:31:59.711 --> 00:32:00.210
minute.

00:33:18.239 --> 00:33:19.250
Hi, everyone.

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:22.330
I'm back to answer your
questions about Cloud TPUs.

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:26.096
So one question here is,
I filed a quota request

00:33:26.096 --> 00:33:27.220
and haven't heard back yet.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:29.720
When will I get a chance
to try Cloud TPUs?

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:32.770
The good news is, as of
recently, you no longer

00:33:32.770 --> 00:33:34.750
need to file a quota request.

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:37.550
Cloud TPU quota is
available to everyone.

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:39.880
So you can just go to
Google Cloud today,

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:42.610
and you should find quota
for at least two Cloud TPUs,

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:43.690
possibly more.

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:47.950
And you can start one up right
now in the US central region.

00:33:47.950 --> 00:33:48.450
Let's see.

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:50.880
There's another
question here, asking

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:56.470
about the performance of TPUs
versus GPUs or other platforms.

00:33:56.470 --> 00:34:00.570
Now, as I mentioned earlier, one
thing that we really recommend

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:03.480
is that you perform your
own benchmark measurements

00:34:03.480 --> 00:34:05.330
on the workloads
that matter to you,

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:08.310
using ideally, our reference
models that give you

00:34:08.310 --> 00:34:11.236
this great starting
point, but in any case,

00:34:11.236 --> 00:34:13.110
we really emphasize that
you should benchmark

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:16.770
with realistic training
data, so you're

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:19.000
measuring the actual
accuracy along the way,

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:21.510
not just the samples per second.

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:24.270
And think about the
total time that it

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:26.136
takes to get to an
accuracy, if you're

00:34:26.136 --> 00:34:27.719
measuring training,
or the throughput,

00:34:27.719 --> 00:34:29.100
if you're measuring inference.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:31.980
And also the total
cost on public clouds.

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:35.820
Or if you're working on premise,
the whole capex and opex

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:38.630
to run the machines
and maintain them,

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:40.980
to get a complete
picture of how to compare

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:42.989
one platform to another.

00:34:42.989 --> 00:34:46.590
If you want to look at
TPUs and GPUs specifically,

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:48.510
one of our customers,
RiseML, has

00:34:48.510 --> 00:34:50.400
published a series
of blog posts where

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:54.480
they've looked at TPU and GPU
performance quite carefully.

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:58.640
You can also look at the
Stanford DAWNBench results.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:34:59.950
Let's see.

00:34:59.950 --> 00:35:02.240
Where can I access
these reference models?

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:04.790
So in the TensorFlow
GitHub repository

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:09.580
at GitHub.com/tensorflow,
there's a directory slash TPU,

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:12.306
and a lot of our reference
models are in there.

00:35:12.306 --> 00:35:13.930
As I mentioned during
the talk, though,

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:15.670
there are some of
these that are also

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:18.790
in a different repository, which
is called Tensor2Tensor, where

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.550
the two is a numeral,
and it's all one word.

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:26.140
So keep an eye on our
reference repository in GitHub,

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:28.799
and then also take a look
at the Tensor2Tensor library

00:35:28.799 --> 00:35:31.090
for the real cutting edge of
these sequence to sequence

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:33.110
models.

00:35:33.110 --> 00:35:34.630
This is a great question.

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:37.530
Can I do inference on
TPUs, or only training?

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:39.960
TPUs support both
training and inference.

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:43.470
At the moment, our software
stack is continuing to mature,

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:47.820
and I'd really recommend
training and batch inference.

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:50.870
Over time, as we continue to
optimize the software stack,

00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:53.790
TPUs should be great for
low latency inference too,

00:35:53.790 --> 00:35:56.280
but I would wait just a little
bit longer before diving

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:57.910
into that.

00:35:57.910 --> 00:35:59.050
And now, somebody's asked--

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.133
well, wait, this is the
second generation of TPUs.

00:36:01.133 --> 00:36:02.410
Is there a third generation?

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:04.300
And so, as I mentioned
through the talk,

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:06.550
we revealed the
third generation TPU

00:36:06.550 --> 00:36:10.240
at Google I/O. It's liquid
cooled, we assemble them,

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:12.250
it's a whole new chip
in a whole new system

00:36:12.250 --> 00:36:14.530
that we assemble into
much larger pods that

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:18.250
deliver more than 100 petaflops
of compute, collectively.

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:22.760
So we're pushing the frontier
forward, as far as we can,

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:25.720
to enable us to train
even larger and more

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:29.680
powerful and accurate machine
learning models that will then

00:36:29.680 --> 00:36:31.500
benefit all the users
of Google products

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:34.850
through all our
different applications.

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:36.850
Let's see if there are
any other questions here.

00:36:39.681 --> 00:36:40.180
Great.

00:36:40.180 --> 00:36:44.800
So one other thing that
you might wonder about

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:53.135
is how to do interactive
development with these Cloud

00:36:53.135 --> 00:36:54.010
TPUs.

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:57.970
And one thing that
I'd recommend would

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:05.550
be do as much as you can
with layers and estimators

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:09.270
and the data sets
API on a Cloud TPU

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:12.330
before you think about scaling
up into a slice of a pod.

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:16.410
So the experience that we're
really trying to build towards

00:37:16.410 --> 00:37:19.650
is one where you
can inexpensively

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:23.970
treat one of these Cloud TPUs
as your personal supercomputer

00:37:23.970 --> 00:37:26.822
in the cloud, that you can
just sit on-- instead of having

00:37:26.822 --> 00:37:28.530
to worry about a
machine under your desk,

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:30.780
you just log into the
cloud and pay only

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:33.420
for what you're using as
you do your development.

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:35.940
And then when you're
really ready to scale up,

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:38.400
just by changing the batch
size, suddenly you're

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:43.920
able to transform days into
hours or hours into minutes

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:46.350
and get a lot more done
in a day than you used

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:47.760
to be able to do in a week.

00:37:47.760 --> 00:37:50.880
So we're building a
whole new paradigm

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:55.170
for machine learning research,
development, and production

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:56.460
here with Cloud TPUs.

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:59.140
And we invite all of you
to join us on this journey

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:01.090
and try out Cloud TPUs today.

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:02.170
Thanks very much.

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:04.440
Stay tuned for the
next session, which is

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:07.920
Make Your Applications Fast and
Reliable with the Stackdriver

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:08.880
API.

